<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reform Judaism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Liberal Judaism, Progressive Judaism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, also known as Liberal or Progressive Judaism, is a major Jewish denomination that emphasises the evolving nature of Judaism, the superiority of its ethical aspects to its ceremonial ones, and a continuous revelation. It began in mid-19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where Rabbi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> Geiger and his associates formulated its principles, removing parts of the liturgy such as bodily resurrection and translating much of the prayer into German. Brought to America by German-born rabbis, it flourished there from the 1860s to the 1930s, the era of Classical Reform. The World Union for Progressive Judaism, founded in 1926, estimates it represents at least 1.8 million people in 50 countries.</p></div>
